    "gate": {
      "period_s": 0.000005,
      "pwm_hz": 1000,
      "carrier": "Let g=floor(relative_tick/gate_ticks), k=g mod 200. The carrier is 1−k/50 for k≤100 and k/50−3 otherwise. Upper closes on applied reference ≥ carrier, lower is complementary. Fine-grid midpoints retain the last gate.",
      "publication": "Use actual solved samples. A command computed at a 100 µs inner tick first affects the solve at the next 5 µs gate tick; keep the fine-grid midpoint value. The physically applied references are recorded separately from the pending and issued references."
    },
    "equations": [
      "At an inner tick, integrate angle with the previous PLL frequency, then update normalized q-axis feedback. Transform using the solved source voltage relative to the station neutral.",
      "Low-pass solved PCC dq every electrical step with backward Euler and the extrapolated source angle. On a 1 ms outer row below the PCC magnitude floor, zero the references and leave the voltage PI integrator unchanged.",
      "On an enabled outer row, A uses P*/(1.5|V_PCC|). B uses signed PI(400−Vdc) with conditional integration. Clip the active current to ±4 A. Compute q_PCC=−Q*/(1.5|V_PCC|) and clip by the remaining current radius, then rotate to the source frame.",
      "Use the newly calculated outer request on the same solved inner row. Use existing dq feedforward and current PI with 100 Hz bandwidth and total phase series R/L. Limit the output vector at .95*(Vdc/2) and roll back both current integrators when saturated. Inverse transform and divide by the measured half-bus voltage.",
      "At 1 ms outer ticks from 200 ms through 220 ms, add 400 W to the 250 W steady command by a linear ramp at station A. Relative tick 200 ms adds zero and 220 ms adds 400 W. Station B sets its active current from signed voltage PI throughout.",
      "P=1.5*(vd*id+vq*iq) and Q=1.5*(vq*id−vd*iq) use measured PCC voltages and phase currents. Each three-cycle window is half-open on the original electrical grid. The DC send and receive powers are the dot products of the two measured pole-to-ground voltages and consistently oriented rail currents at each end."
    ]
